00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:39.000
They're actually more complex than we realize. And the worry is that many of these technological solutions to environmental problems only solve a piece of the problem, without dealing with the overall health of the ecosystem. You might be able to get the fish up the river, but the river itself is still broken. With that in mind, people have recently started calling on the government to remove old dams and return entire river systems to their free flowing state. The idea that you could actually take a major dam out 20 years ago was crazy talk. I mean, that was just not something that people would talk about as a serious political or social possibility.
它们实际上比我们意识到的要复杂得多。令人担忧的是，许多针对环境问题的技术解决方案只是解决了问题的一部分，而没有解决整个生态系统的健康问题。你或许能够把鱼送上河流，但河流本身仍然是破碎的。有鉴于此，人们最近开始呼吁政府拆除老旧大坝，恢复整个河流系统的自由流淌状态。20 years ago, you could actually take a major dam out. 20年前，你如果说要拆除一座大型大坝，那简直是疯了。我的意思是，那根本不是人们会作为严肃的政治或社会可能性来讨论的事情。

28
00:12:39.000 --> 00:13:09.000
But now it's happening. After years of protest by indigenous people and conservation groups, dams have come down on the Sandy River in Oregon and the L.W.A. River in Washington State, and the salmon have begun to come back. We're not going to get rid of every dam dam in the country, but a lot of them are reaching the end of their structural lifetimes, and some are just not profitable anymore. And in those cases, David wants us to look at each one on a case-by-case basis and ask, well what about this damn?
但现在它正在发生。经过原住民和环保组织多年的抗议，俄勒冈州的桑迪河和华盛顿州的勒埃瓦河上的大坝已经被拆除，鲑鱼已经开始回归。我们不会拆除国内的每一座大坝，但其中许多大坝正在接近其结构寿命的终点，有些则根本不再盈利。在这些情况下，大卫希望我们逐个情况进行评估，并问问：那么这座大坝呢？

29
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:51.000
Does this one still make sense? Todd Delegant of Wush supports taking out old, unprofitable dams, but a concern for anti-dam activists is that technology is like the salmon cannon, could slow down these efforts, or even enable more dams to be built because companies can say, well don't worry about the salmon migration, people have designed solutions to that problem. And that's a worry with wildlife corridors more generally. Wildlife corridors cannot be a substitution for preservation of key habitat areas. That's Louise Mozingo again from UC Berkeley. The reconnection of fragmented landscapes should not be an excuse to destroy existing really
这座大坝现在还有意义吗？WUSH的托德·德莱甘支持拆除老旧且不盈利的大坝，但反大坝活动人士的一个担忧是，像鲑鱼大炮这样的技术可能会延缓这些努力，甚至允许建造更多的大坝，因为公司可以宣称：“哦，不用担心鲑鱼洄游，人们已经为这个问题设计了解决方案。”这也是更广泛意义上对野生动物走廊的担忧。野生动物走廊不能替代对关键栖息地区域的保护。再次引用加州大学伯克利分校的路易丝·马辛戈的话。重新连通碎片化的景观不应该成为破坏现有的真正

30
00:13:51.000 --> 00:14:21.000
high quality habitats. We have too few of them and we need to preserve the ones that are functioning really well that are supporting large and diverse wildlife populations. Corridors only really make sense, she says, in places where habitats have already been fragmented. And now you can knit them back together in ways that support, if not a robust population, an adequate population for the species to survive. And in those situations where humans have already had a huge impact on the landscape,
高品质栖息地的借口。我们拥有的这类栖息地太少了，我们需要保护那些运作良好、正在支撑庞大且多样化野生动物种群的栖息地。她表示，走廊只有在栖息地已经被碎片化的地方才有真正的意义。现在你可以将它们重新编织在一起，其方式能够支持——即使不是一个强健的种群，也是一个足以让物种生存的种群。在人类已经对景观产生巨大影响的那些情况下，

31
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:59.000
design for the benefit of non-humans could be the key to keeping wildlife around. We spent most of the 20th century carving up and dividing the landscape with impassable objects made of asphalt in concrete. But if you zoom in, you can see the effort to stitch together habitats with the tiniest of threads that could make all the difference. A crab ladder here, a salamander tunnel there, and that's great. But if we're honest, we can't fix every problem we created by building something new. For a species like salmon to survive in the modern world, it's going to take a patchwork approach of addition and subtraction.
为了非人类的利益进行设计，可能是让野生动物留存下来的关键。我们在整个20世纪的大部分时间里，都在用由沥青和混凝土制成的不可逾越的物体切割和划分景观。但如果你放大镜头，你会发现人们正致力于用最细微的线索将栖息地缝合在一起，而这可能会带来巨大的改变。这里有一个螃蟹梯，那里有一条火蜥蜴隧道，这很棒。但坦白说，我们无法通过建造新事物来解决我们所产生的所有问题。对于像鲑鱼这样要在现代世界中生存的物种来说，这将需要采取增减结合的拼凑方法。

32
00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:49.000
